Use these commands in your DevOps workflow:

1. Check before you change

• git status
→ Check your branch and changed files.

• git diff
→ Review unstaged changes.

• git diff --staged
→ Review your next commit. Check for secrets and unwanted edits.

2. Check remote updates

• git fetch origin
→ Download updates without merging them.

• git log --oneline HEAD..origin/main
→ See commits on remote main missing from your current branch.

• git pull --ff-only
→ Update from your upstream branch; stop if histories diverge.

3. Prepare a focused fix

• git switch -c fix/health-check
→ Create a branch from your current commit. Check your starting point first.

• git add -p
→ Stage selected changes.

• git commit -m "Fix health-check endpoint"
→ Record the staged fix.

• git push -u origin fix/health-check
→ Publish your branch, then open a pull request for review and CI.

4. Investigate the failure

• git log --oneline --graph --decorate -15
→ Inspect recent history.

• git show <commit-sha>
→ Examine a suspected commit.

• git diff <good-sha> <bad-sha> -- deployment.yaml
→ Compare the file between working and failing revisions.

Replace placeholders with actual commit IDs and your file path.

5. Reverse a shared change

• git revert <commit-sha>
→ Create a new commit that reverses an earlier change while preserving history.

Conflicts may need resolution. Reverting merge commits requires extra care.

Interview scenario:
“A bad commit reached main. What would you do?”

“Identify the deployed commit, inspect the change, prepare a revert through review and CI, then deploy and verify recovery.”

Remember: a Git revert alone does not roll back a running application.

Practise this habit:
Inspect → Change → Review → Verify.
